#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# Unlike the prompt commands here, this one is not a wrapper around
# lib/enclave-cmd.sh: it reads Enclave's host-side state and prints a report
# instead of starting a session, so it handles its own arguments -- including
# the -h that enclave would otherwise pass through to a tool. It is Python
# rather than sh because the report aggregates gigabytes of JSONL transcripts,
# network logs, and SQLite databases, which a shell pipeline cannot do in
# reasonable time.
#
# Read-only by construction. It opens an allowlist of known transcript, log,
# and metadata files for reading and nothing else: env stores, auth stores,
# secrets, TLS material, and the tools' credential files are never opened.
# Below each root no symlink is followed, so a link planted in a store cannot
# lead the scan elsewhere. SQLite databases are opened immutable, or -- when a
# write-ahead log holds data the main file does not yet have -- copied into a
# private temporary directory first, so a live tool's database, its WAL, and
# its shared-memory index are never touched. OpenCode's database also keeps its
# account tokens, so that copy sits in a 0700 directory, under
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR where there is one, and is removed before the command
# exits. The copy and the report are the only things this command writes.

